//! ## Character-change select lifecycle (`0x80601BE0..0x80602FF0`)
//!
//! These are REL `d_basesNP` evidence addresses on the canonical synthetic base
//! `0x80600000`; they are **not** runtime addresses. See the cluster comment
//! in this section and `docs/REL.md`.
//!
//! | address | size | Rust | plain-English role |
//! |---|---|---|---|
//! | `0x80601CD0` | 64 B | [`fn_80601cd0`] | array-element delete helper |
//! | `0x80601EF0` | 96 B | [`fn_80601ef0`] | delete helper with a member at `+0x18` |
//! | `0x80602C20` | 328 B | [`fn_80602c20`] | apply the selected character preview |
//! | `0x80602EE0` | 180 B | [`fn_80602ee0`] | final slot confirmation |
//! | `0x80602FB0` | 64 B | [`fn_80602fb0`] | start the confirm animation |
//! | `0x80602FF0` | 88 B | [`fn_80602ff0`] | notify when the layout animation finished |
//!
//! # Why this code exists in the image
//!
//! The sequence-player cluster is the player half of the NintendoWare sound
//! library. `0x803FC990` is
//! the table header in BSS, `0x803FC9C8` (= header + `0x38`) is slot 0, and the
//! stride is `0xA8`. `0x80270AD0` (not translated here) lazily clears and
//! numbers the four slots.
//!
//! ## Slot layout
//!
//! | slot offset | meaning (`// inferred:` from all uses) |
//! |---|---|
//! | `+0x00` | started flag |
//! | `+0x01` | "busy this update" flag |
//! | `+0x02` | enable the parameter scan |
//! | `+0x03` | one-shot pending flag |
//! | `+0x04` | completion callback enabled |
//! | `+0x05` | async operation in flight |
//! | `+0x06` / `+0x07` | alarm A / alarm B armed |
//! | `+0x08` | state (0/2/4/6 idle, 1/3/5 queued) |
//! | `+0x0C` / `+0x10` | request / fallback request |
//! | `+0x14` | 0x20-byte parameter block |
//! | `+0x34` | slot index 0..=3 (also the async handle) |
//! | `+0x38` | completion callback |
//! | `+0x40` / `+0x70` | two `OSAlarm` objects (`0x30` bytes each) |
//! | `+0xA0` | `u64` time-base snapshot |
//!
//! Flow: [`fn_802701a0`] / [`fn_80270260`] write a request â†’
//! [`fn_80270330`] maps it to `0x801E1E20(handle, mode, completion)` â†’ the
//! completion lands in [`fn_80270720`] / [`fn_80270830`] (or the untranslated
//! `0x80270920`) â†’ the periodic tick (`0x80270B80` â†’ `0x80270C20`) calls
//! [`fn_80270330`] for every slot and [`fn_80270410`] for slots in state 4.
//!
//! [`fn_80270000`] / [`fn_80270060`] are a separate pool pair used by that
//! machinery to stage buffers; they sit in the same function run and are called
//! from the same update path.
//!
//! The gameplay actor cluster is mostly reading: it classifies the actor's pose
//! and speed, copies one of three nine-float pose blocks for callers, clamps
//! `+0x10C`/`+0x110` from the movement state, and in several cases binds the
//! actor's owner index and world position into the shared effect spawners
//! `0x8008FB60` / `0x8008FBA0` / `0x8008FC80`. No renderer state is touched.
//!
//! The character-change select cluster is the tail of the `d_basesNP` screen
//! code that was not part of the wave-2 slice in [`crate::game::bases`]. It
//! covers the delete helpers installed for the screen's 4-element array, the
//! preview-application step, the final slot confirmation, and two layout
//! animation calls. It reads the same select-screen object (`+0x228` interface,
//! `+0x2D8` selected slot, `+0x2D4` preview index) and dispatches state objects
//! by their synthetic-base addresses.
//!
//! # Start here
//!
//! For the sequence player, read [`fn_80270000`] first: nine lines, and it
//! introduces the pool header (`+0x0C` aligned start, `+0x10` end, `+0x14`
//! cursor) that [`fn_80270060`] then flushes. Next read [`fn_80270120`] â†’
//! [`fn_802701a0`] â†’ [`fn_80270330`] â†’ [`fn_80270410`] for the slot state
//! machine, and finally [`fn_80270b70`] + [`fn_80270720`] to see how a
//! completion callback finds "its" slot.
//!
//! For the gameplay actor, read [`fn_8004b160`] first: the smallest predicate,
//! and it shows the float constants and the `+0x2A8` flag byte. Then read
//! [`fn_8004bf80`], which turns the three-way pose state into a nine-float
//! copy - together they are the clearest description of the actor's pose
//! without touching the renderer. For the wave-3 motion helpers, continue with
//! [`fn_8004b460`] (the `Â±4.0` direction scale) and [`fn_8004b640`] (the speed
//! clamp); for effects, [`fn_8004ce70`] is the complete seven-argument example.
//! Wave 4 starts at [`fn_8004bac0`] (the dispatcher that all speed branches
//! pass through), then [`fn_8004c480`] â†’ [`fn_8004c560`] /
//! [`fn_8004c620`] for the landing-speed tables. The three event-flag leaves
//! [`fn_80056c70`] / [`fn_80056cb0`] / [`fn_80056cf0`] explain the gate names
//! used throughout this section.
//!
//! For the wave-91 spawn-slot helpers, read [`fn_8005ffb0`] first (four handle
//! tests), then [`fn_8005fef0`] (the same handles filtered by the `{0,1,3,2}`
//! character map) and [`fn_8005fd20`] (the occupied-but-idle count). The
//! ride-momentum block starts at [`fn_80053aa0`], is filled by
//! [`fn_80053940`] and is spent by [`fn_80053ad0`]; [`fn_80053e60`] is the
//! clearest example of the two-float effect slot `+0x3FC`.
//!
//! For wave 92, read [`fn_800414e0`] first: it is the four-record
//! `daFireBall` state installer and shows the permutation the compiler applied
//! to every nine-word parameter block. Then [`fn_800460d0`] (the whole actor
//! constructor, and therefore the object map) and [`fn_800467c0`] (the same
//! fields torn down again). [`fn_80046fc0`] is the smallest example of an
//! event-flag reset run.
//!
//! For the select-screen lifecycle, read [`fn_80601cd0`] first (the smallest
//! delete helper), then [`fn_80602fb0`] (one layout animation call), and finally
//! [`fn_80602c20`] to see the full preview-application branch.
//!
//! # The outlined-prologue artefact (important when reading the C)
//!
//! Several sequence-player functions start with `bl 0x802DCD6C`,
//! `bl 0x802DCD68` or `bl 0x802DCD74` and end with `bl 0x802DCDB8` (and
//! friends). Those are **not** logic calls. `0x802DCD40..0x802DCD88` is a
//! family of register-save entry points that share one `blr`, and
//! `0x802DCD8C..0x802DCDD4` is the mirroring restore family:
//!
//! ```text
//! 0x802DCD40  stw r14,-0x48(r11) ... stw r31,-4(r11) ; blr   (save r14..r31)
//! 0x802DCD6C  stw r25,-0x1C(r11) ... stw r31,-4(r11) ; blr   (save r25..r31)
//! 0x802DCDB8  lwz r25,-0x1C(r11) ... lwz r31,-4(r11) ; blr   (restore r25..r31)
//! ```
//!
//! Entering in the middle works because every entry point falls through to the
//! same `blr`. Ghidra mis-models each `stw` as a 4-byte function, and models the
//! `bl` as a call returning a 64-bit value in `r3:r4`; the decompiled `u64`
//! therefore really means "the argument registers are still live". This module
//! omits those spills (they only touch callee-saved registers) and reads the
//! incoming arguments directly. [`fn_80270410`] is the function where this
//! artefact changes the apparent signature.
//!
//! # Dependencies
//!
//! * [`crate::platform`] - big-endian volatile loads/stores and indirect calls.
//!   The host-side layer covers every arity this module needs (`call0` through
//!   `call7`, plus `call_u64`). The only local helpers are `call0_u32`, the
//!   result-preserving form of `platform::call0` (the interrupt lock, manager
//!   pointer and player-table initialiser all return a `u32`), `read_i8` for
//!   sign-extended owner indices, the older actor cluster's `read_f32` /
//!   `write_f32` wrappers, and the two vtable-call helpers `call_actor_slot` /
//!   `call_interface_slot`. `platform` also provides `read_f32` / `write_f32` /
//!   `read_i32`, used by the wave-3 functions.
//! * Everything outside these address ranges is called by absolute address and
//!   is **not** translated here: the OS interrupt/alarm API (`0x801AB2E0`,
//!   `0x801AB540`, `0x801AB5B0`, `0x801AB640`, `0x801ABA70`, `0x801ABA80`,
//!   `0x801B1140`, `0x801B1180`, `0x801B5F80`), the async-op layer
//!   (`0x801E1E20`, `0x801E35F0`, `0x801E3680`), the `memset` wrapper
//!   `0x800046B4`, the pool mutex (`0x801B2E60` / `0x801B2F40` via the manager
//!   pointer `0x8027B5F0`), the streaming range calls (`0x80268C60`,
//!   `0x80268D90`, `0x80268EC0`), the parameter-block builder `0x80227EF0`, and
//!   the sound helpers `0x80270AD0` / `0x80270C20` / `0x80270920` (all three
//!   still untranslated; see the coverage doc).
//! * The audio container decoded by this table lives in
//!   [`crate::resource::archive`] and [`crate::audio::seq`].
//! * The gameplay actor cluster calls untranslated helpers by absolute
//!   address: the effect spawners `0x8008FB60`, `0x8008FBA0`, `0x8008FBF0`,
//!   `0x8008FC40` and `0x8008FC80`, the animation-controller accessors
//!   `0x80070720` / `0x80070760` / `0x800707E0` / `0x80070820`, the
//!   model/transform getter `0x80057650`, the movement-state bit tests
//!   `0x8005E4D0`..`0x8005E520` / `0x8005E610` / `0x8005E6F0` /
//!   `0x8005E740` / `0x8005E7A0`, the movement-mode setter `0x8005E460`, the
//!   event-flag accessors `0x80056CF0` / `0x80056C70`, the scene-position
//!   query `0x800D5790`, the effect-height helper `0x8006CED0`, the scene test
//!   `0x800CD2C0`, the sound call `0x80057F60`, the slip helpers
//!   `0x80047B70` / `0x80047EE0`, and the untranslated speed/pose helper
//!   `0x8004BC80`. It also reads the animation-rate tables at `0x802EE978`,
//!   `0x802EE9B4`, `0x802EE9F0`, `0x802EEA2C`, `0x802EEAC0`, `0x802EEAD8` and
//!   `0x802EEAE4`, and the effect-name tables at `0x80307D8C`, `0x80307F18`,
//!   `0x80308188`, `0x80308248` and `0x803082B4`. None of them touch this
//!   module's sequence-player table.
//! * The select-screen cluster calls main.dol helpers `0x8015F800`,
//!   `0x8015F9F0`, `0x802B9280`, `0x80195380`, `0x800C92F0` and `0x800C9610`,
//!   and reuses the already-translated [`crate::game::bases::fn_80602460`],
//!   [`crate::game::bases::fn_806027c0`] and
//!   [`crate::game::bases::fn_806027e0`]. The REL-local addresses are evidence
//!   addresses on the synthetic base, not runtime pointers.
//!
//! # Glossary (first use)
//!
//! * **DOL** - the Wii executable container `sys/main.dol`; addresses here are
//!   its *virtual* addresses (image base `0x80004000`), not file offsets.
//! * **BSS** - zero-initialised memory at `0x80351680..0x8042FC3C`; the player
//!   table at `0x803FC990` lives there.
//! * **NW4R / nw4hbm** - NintendoWare 4 Revolution, the SDK layer this sound
//!   code belongs to (see `docs/GLOSSARY.md`).
//! * **OSAlarm** - the SDK's 0x30-byte timer object (`OSCreateAlarm`,
//!   `OSSetAlarm`, `OSSetPeriodicAlarm`, `OSCancelAlarm`).
//! * **OS_BUS_CLOCK** - the `u32` time-base frequency at `0x800000F8`.
//! * **r13 / SDA** - the PowerPC small-data base; this cluster explicitly
//!   passes its bases, so no `unaff_r13` appears below.
//! * **Slot** - one of the four `0xA8`-byte records: `// inferred:` one sequence
//!   player each (the four players of the sound system).
//! * **Player actor** - the gameplay object that owns a world position at
//!   `+0xAC`, an animation controller at `+0x1EC`, an owning-player index at
//!   `+0x38D` and state flags at `+0x10D4`/`+0x10D8`. Distinct from the sound
//!   "sequence player".
//! * **Effect spawner** - `0x8008FB60` / `0x8008FBA0` / `0x8008FC80`, the
//!   shared binders that store a player index in an SDA global, call the effect
//!   system (`0x8016C890` or the indirect `+0xA0` slot), and restore the global
//!   to `-1`.
//! * **REL** - a relocatable module loaded beside `main.dol`; addresses in
//!   `d_basesNP` are reconstructed on the canonical synthetic base
//!   `0x80600000`. See `docs/REL.md`.
//! * **Character-change select** - the `d_basesNP` screen that lets each player
//!   choose a character. Its object holds a layout at `+0x90`, an interface at
//!   `+0x228`, four player-view pointers at `+0x80`, and the selected slot at
//!   `+0x2D8`.
//! * **Synthetic base** - the fixed address `0x80600000` assigned by
//!   `tools/rel2elf.mjs` for static analysis; not the runtime load address.
//!
//! # Status and provenance
//!
//! Promoted from `src/subsystems/r80260_802ed.rs` (thirteen sound functions)
//! and `src/subsystems/r80040_80080.rs` (eight gameplay actor functions). Wave 3
//! adds seventeen gameplay-actor functions from the `0x8004B290..0x8004D5A0`
//! run and seven select-screen functions from REL `d_basesNP`; their ledger rows
//! are in `ledger/agents/wave3-player.csv`. Wave 4 adds twenty-three
//! gameplay-actor functions from `0x8004BAC0..0x80057AD0`; their rows are in
//! `ledger/agents/wave4-player.csv`. Wave 5 adds twenty-three gameplay-actor
//! functions from `0x8004A2C0..0x8004F700`; their rows are in
//! `ledger/agents/wave5-player.csv`. Wave 39 adds thirty gameplay-actor
//! functions from `0x800534B0..0x8005D090`; their rows are in
//! `ledger/agents/wave39-player.csv`. The ledger module for all
//! ninety-one rows is `game/player`. Every row keeps its `# Original` evidence
//! block; this is a move, not a re-implementation.

use crate::game::bases::{
    interface_call, interface_call2, interface_call3, interface_call_desc, interface_call_f32_1,
    Descriptor,
};
use crate::platform::{self, call4, call5, call6, call7, call_u64};
use crate::game::layouts::DActor;

// ===========================================================================
// Absolute addresses and field offsets used by the player table.
//
// The shipped image has no symbol table; names below are either (a) plain
// addresses, or (b) `// inferred:` role names justified at the definition.
// ===========================================================================

/// Base of the four-slot player table in BSS.
///
/// `// inferred:` `0x80270AD0` lazily clears 4 records of `0xA8` bytes starting
/// at `0x803FC9C8` and stamps them with indices `0..=3`; `0x80270C20` walks the
/// same four records. `0x803FC990` is the table header, `0x803FC9C8` is
/// `header + 0x38`, which is exactly the bias in [`fn_80270b70`].
const PLAYER_TABLE: usize = 0x803F_C990;

/// Offset of the first slot from [`PLAYER_TABLE`] (`0x803FC9C8`).
const SLOT_FIRST: usize = 0x38;

/// Distance between two slot records (168 bytes).
const SLOT_STRIDE: usize = 0xA8;

/// Number of slots (`// observed:` all loops use 4).
const SLOT_COUNT: u32 = 4;

/// Slot byte `+0x00`: "object has been started" (set to 1 by start, 0 by stop).
const SLOT_STARTED: usize = 0x00;
/// Slot byte `+0x01`: "the update loop currently considers the slot busy".
const SLOT_RUNNING: usize = 0x01;
/// Slot byte `+0x02`: enables the parameter scan in [`fn_80270410`].
const SLOT_SCAN_ENABLE: usize = 0x02;
/// Slot byte `+0x03`: one-shot flag consumed by [`fn_80270410`].
const SLOT_PENDING: usize = 0x03;
/// Slot byte `+0x04`: "completion callback may fire".
const SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE: usize = 0x04;
/// Slot byte `+0x05`: "an async operation is in flight".
const SLOT_BUSY: usize = 0x05;
/// Slot byte `+0x06`: alarm A (at `+0x40`) is armed.
const SLOT_ALARM_A: usize = 0x06;
/// Slot byte `+0x07`: alarm B (at `+0x70`) is armed.
const SLOT_ALARM_B: usize = 0x07;
/// Slot word `+0x08`: state, `0` or an even number while idle, odd while queued.
const SLOT_STATE: usize = 0x08;
/// Slot word `+0x0C`: pending request, consumed by [`fn_80270330`].
const SLOT_REQUEST: usize = 0x0C;
/// Slot word `+0x10`: fallback request used when `+0x0C` is zero.
const SLOT_REQUEST_ALT: usize = 0x10;
/// Slot `0x20`-byte parameter block at `+0x14` (cleared by `memset` on success).
const SLOT_PARAMS: usize = 0x14;
/// Slot word `+0x34`: small index (`0..=3`) used as the async-op handle.
const SLOT_INDEX: usize = 0x34;
/// Slot word `+0x38`: completion callback, or 0.
const SLOT_COMPLETION: usize = 0x38;
/// Slot `OSAlarm` A at `+0x40` (`OSAlarm` is `0x30` bytes).
const SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ: usize = 0x40;
/// Slot `OSAlarm` B at `+0x70`.
const SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ: usize = 0x70;
/// Slot `u64` time-base snapshot at `+0xA0` (high word first).
const SLOT_TIME: usize = 0xA0;

/// `u32` bus-clock frequency at `0x800000F8` (OS global, `OS_BUS_CLOCK`).
const OS_BUS_CLOCK: usize = 0x8000_00F8;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Callees outside this lane (called by absolute address).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `0x801B1140` - interrupt lock, returns the previous interrupt state.
///
/// `// inferred:` every caller saves the result and hands it back to
/// `0x801B1180`, which is the `OSDisableInterrupts`/`OSRestoreInterrupts`
/// contract.
const IRQ_DISABLE: usize = 0x801B_1140;
/// `0x801B1180` - interrupt restore (takes the token from [`IRQ_DISABLE`]).
const IRQ_RESTORE: usize = 0x801B_1180;
/// `0x801B5F80` - `OSGetTime`: returns a 64-bit time-base value.
const OS_GET_TIME: usize = 0x801B_5F80;

/// `0x801AB2E0` - `OSCreateAlarm`-shaped: zeroes the two words of an alarm.
///
/// `// observed:` the callee is literally `*p = 0; p[1] = 0`.
const ALARM_INIT: usize = 0x801A_B2E0;
/// `0x801ABA70` - store a user-data word at `alarm + 0x28`.
const ALARM_SET_USER: usize = 0x801A_BA70;
/// `0x801ABA80` - load the user-data word from `alarm + 0x28`.
const ALARM_GET_USER: usize = 0x801A_BA80;
/// `0x801AB640` - `OSCancelAlarm`-shaped: stops an alarm.
const ALARM_CANCEL: usize = 0x801A_B640;
/// `0x801AB540` - one-shot alarm setter. Arguments (as Ghidra shows them):
/// alarm, unused, delta-high, delta-low, handler.
const ALARM_SET: usize = 0x801A_B540;
/// `0x801AB5B0` - periodic alarm setter. Seven arguments, see [`fn_80270b80`].
const ALARM_SET_PERIODIC: usize = 0x801A_B5B0;

/// `0x801E1E20` - start an async operation: `(handle, mode, completion)`.
///
/// `// inferred:` the first argument comes back as the completion callback's
/// first parameter (see [`fn_80270720`]), so it is an opaque operation handle.
const ASYNC_START: usize = 0x801E_1E20;
/// `0x801E35F0` - async-op query; `0` means "cannot submit" in [`fn_80270410`].
const ASYNC_READY: usize = 0x801E_35F0;
/// `0x801E3680` - async-op submit: `(handle, params, len)`, non-zero = accepted.
const ASYNC_SUBMIT: usize = 0x801E_3680;

/// `0x800046B4` - `memset` wrapper: `(dst, value, len)`.
const MEMSET: usize = 0x8000_46B4;

/// `0x8027_B5F0` - global manager pointer used to reach the mutex at `+0x354`.
const MANAGER: usize = 0x8027_B5F0;
/// `0x801B2E60` / `0x801B2F40` - lock / unlock the manager's mutex at `+0x354`.
const MUTEX_LOCK: usize = 0x801B_2E60;
const MUTEX_UNLOCK: usize = 0x801B_2F40;
/// `0x80268C60` - global streaming handle used by the two cache operations.
const STREAM_HANDLE: usize = 0x8026_8C60;
/// `0x80268D90` / `0x80268EC0` - range store / range flush:
/// `(handle, address, length, 0)`. `// inferred:` from the identical argument
/// shape and their use on a just-written buffer.
const STREAM_STORE: usize = 0x8026_8D90;
const STREAM_FLUSH: usize = 0x8026_8EC0;
/// `0x8022_7EF0` - build the async parameter block used by [`fn_80270410`].
const BUILD_PARAMS: usize = 0x8022_7EF0;
/// `0x80270AD0` - lazily initialise [`PLAYER_TABLE`] and return its address.
///
/// `// unknown:` not translated in this pass; its body is visible in
/// `80270ad0_FUN_80270ad0.c` (4 slots, indices 0..=3) and is the first item of
/// the next-target list.
const PLAYER_TABLE_GET: usize = 0x8027_0AD0;

// ===========================================================================
// Pool + four-slot player state machine, 0x80270000..0x80270B80
// ===========================================================================

/// Reserves a window inside a pool header.
///
/// The header keeps three words: `+0x0C` aligned start, `+0x10` end, `+0x14`
/// current cursor. This routine aligns `base` up to 32 bytes and refuses the
/// reservation when the aligned start no longer fits in `base + size`. On
/// success it publishes all three words and returns 1; on refusal it writes
/// nothing.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270000` at `0x80270000` (48 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270000_FUN_80270000.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `pool`: pool header address (three writable words).
/// - `base`: requested start offset.
/// - `size`: requested length.
///
/// # Returns
/// `1` when reserved, `0` when the aligned start overflows the window.
///
/// # Side effects
/// writes `pool + 0x0C`, `+0x10`, `+0x14` on success only.
///
/// # Safety
/// `pool` must point to at least `0x18` writable bytes.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - every operation is a plain store/compare.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270000(pool: usize, base: u32, size: u32) -> u32 {
    let aligned = base.wrapping_add(0x1F) & 0xFFFF_FFE0;
    if size.wrapping_add(base) < aligned {
        return 0;
    }
    platform::write_u32(pool + 0x0C, aligned);
    platform::write_u32(pool + 0x10, size.wrapping_add(base));
    platform::write_u32(pool + 0x14, aligned);
    1
}

/// Flushes everything written into the pool since the last call and rewinds the
/// cursor.
///
/// Two range operations run on `[+0x0C, +0x14)` under the manager mutex: a
/// "store" (`0x80268D90`) and a "flush" (`0x80268EC0`), each taking the streaming
/// handle, the start, the length and a 0. The cursor is then set back to the
/// start, so the region can be reused.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270060` at `0x80270060` (136 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270060_FUN_80270060.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `pool`: pool header with `+0x0C` start and `+0x14` cursor.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - locks/unlocks the mutex at `manager + 0x354`,
/// - two cache operations over the written range,
/// - `pool + 0x14 = pool + 0x0C`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `pool` must be a valid header; the range it describes must be readable.
///
/// # Confidence
/// medium - the two calls and their arguments are explicit, but the roles of
/// `0x80268D90`/`0x80268EC0` are inferred from their identical shape.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270060(pool: usize) {
    let manager = call0_u32(MANAGER);
    // observed: the manager keeps its mutex at +0x354 (same offset used for the
    // unlock below).
    platform::call1(MUTEX_LOCK, (manager + 0x354) as u32);

    let start = platform::read_u32(pool + 0x0C);
    let length = platform::read_u32(pool + 0x14).wrapping_sub(start);
    let handle = call0_u32(STREAM_HANDLE);
    call4(STREAM_STORE, handle, start, length, 0);

    // observed: the header is re-read for the second operation.
    let start = platform::read_u32(pool + 0x0C);
    let length = platform::read_u32(pool + 0x14).wrapping_sub(start);
    let handle = call0_u32(STREAM_HANDLE);
    call4(STREAM_FLUSH, handle, start, length, 0);

    platform::write_u32(pool + 0x14, platform::read_u32(pool + 0x0C));

    let manager = call0_u32(MANAGER);
    platform::call1(MUTEX_UNLOCK, (manager + 0x354) as u32);
}

/// Constructs one `0xA8`-byte player slot: six zero bytes, four zero words,
/// and two initialised `OSAlarm` objects that point back at the slot.
///
/// The slot layout used by the rest of this module is documented on the
/// `SLOT_*` constants. The two alarms live at `+0x40` and `+0x70`; each gets
/// `OSCreateAlarm` (`*p = 0; p[1] = 0`) and `OSSetAlarmUserData(slot)`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270120` at `0x80270120` (128 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270120_FUN_80270120.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `slot`: address of the `0xA8`-byte slot to construct.
///
/// # Returns
/// `slot` (the C returns its first argument).
///
/// # Side effects
/// writes bytes `+0x00..=+0x05`, words `+0x08`, `+0x0C`, `+0x10`, `+0x38`, and
/// initialises both alarm objects.
///
/// # Safety
/// `slot` must be writable for `0xA8` bytes.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the field writes and both alarm initialisations are explicit; the
/// `OSAlarm` identification comes from the standard 0x30-byte layout and the
/// set/get/cancel pattern in the sibling functions.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270120(slot: usize) -> usize {
    for offset in 0..6 {
        platform::write_u8(slot + offset, 0);
    }
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 0);
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST, 0);
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST_ALT, 0);
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION, 0);

    platform::call1(ALARM_INIT, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ) as u32);
    platform::call2(ALARM_SET_USER, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ) as u32, slot as u32);
    platform::call1(ALARM_INIT, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32);
    platform::call2(ALARM_SET_USER, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32, slot as u32);

    slot
}

/// Starts a player slot: cancels both alarms, fires the previous completion
/// callback with `0`, and requests state 1.
///
/// The body runs under the interrupt lock so that a timer cannot observe the
/// half-updated slot. Note the ordering: the old callback is told the operation
/// finished with `0` **after** it has been replaced in memory only at the very
/// end, so the callback still sees itself installed.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_802701a0` at `0x802701A0` (192 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/802701a0_FUN_802701a0.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `slot`: player slot.
/// - `completion`: new completion callback address, or 0 for none. It is stored
///   at `+0x38` and receives `(slot_index, result)`.
///
/// # Returns
/// Always `1`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - cancels alarms A and B (`0x801AB640`),
/// - calls the previous `+0x38` callback with `(*(u32 *)(slot + 0x34), 0)`,
/// - writes `+0x0C = 1`, `+0x38 = completion`, `+0x00 = 1`, and clears the
///   running/callback-enable/flag bytes.
///
/// # Safety
/// `slot` must be a constructed slot; `completion`, when non-zero, must be a
/// function with the `(u32, i32)` signature used by [`fn_80270720`].
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - every store is explicit; state numbering is inferred from
/// [`fn_80270330`], the only consumer of `+0x0C`.
pub unsafe fn fn_802701a0(slot: usize, completion: u32) -> u32 {
    let token = call0_u32(IRQ_DISABLE);

    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING, 0);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_SCAN_ENABLE, 0);
    platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ) as u32);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A, 0);
    platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 0);

    // observed: the C clears +6 and +7 a second time before publishing state.
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A, 0);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING, 0);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_SCAN_ENABLE, 1);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 0);

    let callback = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION);
    if callback != 0 {
        let index = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_INDEX);
        platform::call2(callback as usize, index, 0);
        platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE, 0);
    }

    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST, 1);
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION, completion);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_STARTED, 1);

    platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
    1
}

/// Stops a player slot: cancels both alarms, notifies the completion callback,
/// and requests state 3.
///
/// Same lock/notify shape as [`fn_802701a0`], except that the slot is marked
/// stopped (`+0x00 = 0`) and the new state request is `3` instead of `1`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270260` at `0x80270260` (172 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270260_FUN_80270260.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `slot`: player slot.
/// - `completion`: replacement completion callback, or 0.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - cancels alarms A/B, calls the old `+0x38` callback with `(index, 0)`,
/// - writes `+0x0C = 3`, `+0x38 = completion`, `+0x00 = 0`.
///
/// # Safety
/// As [`fn_802701a0`].
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - identical structure to [`fn_802701a0`] with three different constants.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270260(slot: usize, completion: u32) {
    let token = call0_u32(IRQ_DISABLE);

    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING, 0);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_SCAN_ENABLE, 0);
    platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ) as u32);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A, 0);
    platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 0);

    let callback = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION);
    if callback != 0 {
        let index = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_INDEX);
        platform::call2(callback as usize, index, 0);
        platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE, 0);
    }

    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST, 3);
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION, completion);
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_STARTED, 0);

    platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
}

/// Dispatches a pending start/stop request to the async layer.
///
/// The slot is skipped while `+0x05` (operation in flight) is set. Otherwise the
/// request is taken from `+0x0C`, falling back to `+0x10` when it is zero, and
/// both request words are cleared before dispatch:
///
/// | request | new `+0x08` | async mode | completion |
/// |---|---|---|---|
/// | 1 | 1 | 1 | `0x80270720` |
/// | 2 | 3 | 4 | `0x80270830` |
/// | 3 | 5 | 0 | `0x80270920` |
///
/// It also sets `+0x04` (callback enabled) and `+0x05` (busy) before calling
/// `0x801E1E20(handle, mode, completion)`. Requests other than 1/2/3 are
/// dropped silently.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270330` at `0x80270330` (192 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270330_FUN_80270330.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `slot`: player slot.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - clears `+0x0C` and `+0x10`,
/// - sets `+0x04 = 1`, `+0x05 = 1`, `+0x08` to 1/3/5,
/// - starts an async operation on the handle at `+0x34`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `slot` must be a valid slot; the async layer must accept the handle.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the three arms are explicit. The meaning of "mode" 1/4/0 is unknown.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270330(slot: usize) {
    if platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_BUSY) != 0 {
        return;
    }

    let mut request = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST);
    if request == 0 {
        request = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST_ALT);
    }
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST, 0);
    platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST_ALT, 0);

    let handle = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_INDEX);
    match request {
        1 => {
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE, 1);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_BUSY, 1);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 1);
            platform::call3(ASYNC_START, handle, 1, 0x8027_0720);
        }
        2 => {
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE, 1);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_BUSY, 1);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 3);
            platform::call3(ASYNC_START, handle, 4, 0x8027_0830);
        }
        3 => {
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE, 1);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_BUSY, 1);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 5);
            platform::call3(ASYNC_START, handle, 0, 0x8027_0920);
        }
        _ => {}
    }
}

/// Per-frame update of one player slot (the `state == 4` path only).
///
/// The routine runs from the periodic handler `0x80270C20`, once per slot:
///
/// 1. Return immediately unless `+0x08 == 4`.
/// 2. Decide whether there is work: when `+0x02` is set, scan **20** words at
///    `params` (two unrolled blocks of ten) and treat "any non-zero" as work;
///    when `+0x02` is clear there is never work.
/// 3. If there is work, take the interrupt lock, ask `0x801E35F0` whether the
///    operation can be submitted, build a parameter block
///    (`0x80227EF0(slot + 0x14, flag, params, 0x28, stack)`) and submit it with
///    `0x801E3680(handle, stack, 0x14)`. A successful submit resets the slot to
///    state 0 / request 2 (the "switch to state 2" path) and returns.
/// 4. On a rising edge (`+0x01 == 0` and work) arm alarm A once with a 480 s
///    timeout (`OSSecondsToTicks(480)`) and remember the time base; cancel
///    alarm B.
/// 5. On a falling edge (`+0x01 != 0` and no work) arm alarm B as a one-shot
///    one-second timer (`bus / 4` ticks) and cancel alarm A.
/// 6. Publish `+0x01 = work`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270410` at `0x80270410` (776 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270410_FUN_80270410.c`
/// - The function starts with `bl 0x802DCD6C` and ends with `bl 0x802DCDB8`:
///   the outlined save/restore pair described in the module header. Ghidra
///   models the first call as returning the `r3:r4` it preserved and prints
///   `FUN_80270410(void)`; the real signature is the two arguments below
///   (`// observed:` the only caller, `0x80270CD4`, sets `r3 = slot`,
///   `r4 = stack buffer`).
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `slot`: player slot.
/// - `params`: address of the `0x28`-byte parameter block the caller filled
///   (`// observed:` `0x801A2050(index, buffer, 0x28)`).
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - may reset the slot to state 0, or arm/disarm both alarms,
/// - may call `0x801E3680` to start an async operation,
/// - writes `+0x01` (and `+0x03 = 0` when a submit is attempted).
///
/// # Safety
/// `slot` must be a live slot; `params` must be readable for the 20 words the
/// scan touches (the caller only copies 0x28 bytes - see `# Confidence`).
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the flow, constants and locks are explicit. Both former caveats are
/// settled by the instruction stream:
/// (a) the `FUN_801ab5b0`-shaped call in the falling-edge arm passes
/// `extraout_r4`, but `0x801AB540` never reads its second argument: its only
/// `r4` use at `0x801AB57C` (`addc r6,r28,r4; adde r5,r29,r3`) consumes the
/// `r3:r4` pair returned by `0x801B5FB0`, not the incoming register;
/// (b) the work scan really is 20 words. `0x80270458` sets `CTR = 2`, the body
/// at `0x80270460..0x80270514` tests ten words, and `bdnz 0x80270460` at
/// `0x80270530` repeats with `r6 += 0x28`, so the shipped loop covers
/// `params+0x00..+0x4F` (0x50 bytes); the caller's 0x28-byte fill is what the
/// scan over-reads, and the body reproduces that faithfully.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270410(slot: usize, params: usize) {
    // observed: `bl 0x802DCD6C` - outlined save of r25..r31; no data effect.
    if platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE) != 4 {
        return;
    }

    // observed: only scan when +0x02 is set; "work" means any non-zero word.
    // Two unrolled blocks of ten words: `mtspr CTR,2`, ten `lwz` at
    // `0x80270460..0x80270514`, then `r6 += 0x28` and `bdnz`.
    let mut has_work = false;
    if platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_SCAN_ENABLE) != 0 {
        'scan: for block in 0..2 {
            for word in 0..10 {
                let address = params + (block * 10 + word) * 4;
                if platform::read_u32(address) != 0 {
                    has_work = true;
                    break 'scan;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    let rising_edge = platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING) == 0 && has_work;
    let falling_edge = platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING) != 0 && !has_work;

    if has_work {
        let token = call0_u32(IRQ_DISABLE);
        let handle = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_INDEX);
        if platform::call1(ASYNC_READY, handle) == 0 {
            platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
            return;
        }

        // observed: `flag = countLeadingZeros(byte) >> 5` - 1 when the byte is
        // 0, else 0 - then the byte is cleared before it is used.
        let flag = (platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_PENDING) as u32).leading_zeros() >> 5;
        platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_PENDING, 0);

        let mut built = [0u8; 56];
        let built_addr = built.as_mut_ptr() as usize;
        call5(
            BUILD_PARAMS,
            (slot + SLOT_PARAMS) as u32,
            flag,
            params as u32,
            0x28,
            built_addr as u32,
        );

        if platform::call3(ASYNC_SUBMIT, handle, built_addr as u32, 0x14) != 0 {
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST_ALT, 1);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 0);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING, 0);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_SCAN_ENABLE, 0);
            platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ) as u32);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A, 0);
            platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 0);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A, 0);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING, 0);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_SCAN_ENABLE, 1);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 0);
            platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
            return;
        }
        platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
    }

    if rising_edge {
        let token = call0_u32(IRQ_DISABLE);
        if platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A) == 0 {
            // observed: `(OS_BUS_CLOCK >> 2) * 0x1E0` as a 64-bit tick count.
            // `OSSecondsToTicks(s) = s * (OS_BUS_CLOCK / 4)`, so this is 480 s.
            let bus = platform::read_u32(OS_BUS_CLOCK);
            let delta = ((bus >> 2) as u64) * 0x1E0;
            call5(
                ALARM_SET,
                (slot + SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ) as u32,
                0,
                (delta >> 32) as u32,
                delta as u32,
                0x8027_0A10,
            );
            // observed: `*(u64 *)(slot + 0xA0) = OSGetTime()`.
            let now = call_u64(OS_GET_TIME);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_TIME, (now >> 32) as u32);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_TIME + 4, now as u32);
            platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A, 1);
        }
        platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32);
        platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 0);
        platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
    }

    if falling_edge {
        let token = call0_u32(IRQ_DISABLE);
        platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 1);
        platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32);
        // observed: `FUN_801ab540(alarm + 0x70, r4, 0, bus >> 2, 0x80270A60)`
        // - a one-shot alarm of `bus / 4` ticks, i.e. one second
        // (`OSSecondsToTicks(1)`). The `r4` slot is dead: `0x801AB540` never
        // reads its second argument (see `# Confidence`).
        call5(
            ALARM_SET,
            (slot + SLOT_ALARM_B_OBJ) as u32,
            0,
            0,
            platform::read_u32(OS_BUS_CLOCK) >> 2,
            0x8027_0A60,
        );
        platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
    }

    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_RUNNING, has_work as u8);
    // observed: `bl 0x802DCDB8` - outlined restore of r25..r31; no data effect.
}

/// Completion handler A: applies the result of a "start"/"play" request.
///
/// The callback receives the slot **index** (the `r3` value handed to
/// `0x801E1E20`), so it first resolves `slot = table + index * 0xA8 + 0x38`
/// with [`fn_80270b70`]. Then:
///
/// * `result == 0`: success - set `+0x03 = 1`, clear the 0x20-byte parameter
///   block at `+0x14`, set state `+0x08 = 2` and `+0x10 = 2`;
/// * `result == -2`: keep the state, set `+0x10 = 1`;
/// * `-1`, `-3` or anything else: `+0x08 = 0`.
///
/// Finally, when the result is not 0 or -2, the callback-enabled byte is set
/// and a completion callback is installed, it invokes `callback(index, result)`
/// once and clears the pointer.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270720` at `0x80270720` (268 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270720_FUN_80270720.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `index`: slot index, `0..=3` (the async operation's handle).
/// - `result`: completion code from the async layer (`0`, `-1`, `-2`, `-3`, ...).
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - writes the slot state words, clears the parameter block on success,
/// - may call the slot's `+0x38` callback and then clears it,
/// - clears `+0x05` (busy) last.
///
/// # Safety
/// `index` must be `0..=3`; the player table must be initialised
/// (`0x80270AD0` does that lazily).
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - all branches and stores are explicit; the `result` code meanings are
/// inferred from the branches.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270720(index: u32, result: i32) {
    let table = call0_u32(PLAYER_TABLE_GET);
    let slot = fn_80270b70(table, index) as usize;

    if result == 0 {
        platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_PENDING, 1);
        platform::call3(MEMSET, (slot + SLOT_PARAMS) as u32, 0, 0x20);
        platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 2);
        platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST_ALT, 2);
    } else if result == -2 {
        platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST_ALT, 1);
    } else {
        // observed: -1, -3 and every other code share the same store.
        platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 0);
    }

    if result != 0 && result != -2 {
        let callback = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION);
        if platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE) != 0 && callback != 0 {
            platform::call2(callback as usize, index, result as u32);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION, 0);
        }
    }

    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_BUSY, 0);
}

/// Completion handler B: applies the result of the "mode 4" request.
///
/// Same shape as [`fn_80270720`] but with the state values of the second async
/// arm: success stores `+0x08 = 4`, `-2` stores `+0x10 = 2`, and every other
/// code stores `+0x08 = 0`. The completion callback fires for every result
/// except `-2`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270830` at `0x80270830` (232 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270830_FUN_80270830.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `index`: slot index `0..=3`.
/// - `result`: completion code (`0`, `-1`, `-2`, `-3`, ...).
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// As [`fn_80270720`], with different constants; also clears `+0x05`.
///
/// # Safety
/// As [`fn_80270720`].
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the branch table is explicit.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270830(index: u32, result: i32) {
    let table = call0_u32(PLAYER_TABLE_GET);
    let slot = fn_80270b70(table, index) as usize;

    if result == 0 {
        platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 4);
    } else if result == -2 {
        platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_REQUEST_ALT, 2);
    } else {
        platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_STATE, 0);
    }

    if result != -2 {
        let callback = platform::read_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION);
        if platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_CALLBACK_ENABLE) != 0 && callback != 0 {
            platform::call2(callback as usize, index, result as u32);
            platform::write_u32(slot + SLOT_COMPLETION, 0);
        }
    }

    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_BUSY, 0);
}

/// Alarm A handler: takes the interrupt lock and reads the alarm's user data.
///
/// `// observed:` the handler calls `OSGetAlarmUserData(alarm)` and discards the
/// result. The only effect is the lock/unlock pair around it. This is
/// reproduced verbatim rather than "simplified away": the call is present in
/// the instruction stream at `0x80270A34`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270a10` at `0x80270A10` (72 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270a10_FUN_80270a10.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `alarm`: address of the `OSAlarm` that fired (slot `+0x40`).
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - disables/restores interrupts,
/// - performs one user-data read from `alarm + 0x28`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `alarm` must be a live `OSAlarm` (0x30 bytes) whose `+0x28` word is set.
///
/// # Confidence
/// medium - the two calls are explicit, but the purpose of the discarded read
/// is unknown; it may be a debug build leftover.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270a10(alarm: u32) {
    let token = call0_u32(IRQ_DISABLE);
    platform::call1(ALARM_GET_USER, alarm);
    platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
}

/// Alarm B handler: cancels alarm A and clears both alarm flags.
///
/// Resolves the slot from the alarm's user data, then, if the slot still thinks
/// alarm A is armed, cancels it and clears `+0x06`. It always clears `+0x07`
/// (alarm B) and restores the interrupt state.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270a60` at `0x80270A60` (112 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270a60_FUN_80270a60.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `alarm`: address of the alarm that fired (slot `+0x70`).
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - possibly `OSCancelAlarm(slot + 0x40)`,
/// - writes `slot + 0x06 = 0`, `slot + 0x07 = 0`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `alarm` must be a live `OSAlarm` with its `+0x28` user data pointing at a
/// valid slot.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - every branch and store is explicit; the handler/flag pairing comes
/// from [`fn_80270410`].
pub unsafe fn fn_80270a60(alarm: u32) {
    let token = call0_u32(IRQ_DISABLE);
    let slot = platform::call1(ALARM_GET_USER, alarm) as usize;

    if platform::read_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A) != 0 {
        platform::call1(ALARM_CANCEL, (slot + SLOT_ALARM_A_OBJ) as u32);
        platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_A, 0);
    }
    platform::write_u8(slot + SLOT_ALARM_B, 0);

    platform::call1(IRQ_RESTORE, token);
}

/// Computes the address of player slot `index`.
///
/// `table` is the value returned by `0x80270AD0` (`0x803FC990`); the first slot
/// sits at `+0x38` and the stride is `0xA8`. This 16-byte function is called by
/// all three completion handlers, so it is translated rather than inlined.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270b70` at `0x80270B70` (16 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270b70_FUN_80270b70.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `table`: player-table base (`0x803FC990`).
/// - `index`: slot index; not range-checked here.
///
/// # Returns
/// `table + index * 0xA8 + 0x38`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// None.
///
/// # Safety
/// No memory access; the caller is responsible for a valid index.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - a single `mulli`/`add` sequence, and the caller passes
/// `0x803FC9C8 = 0x803FC990 + 0x38` as the loop start.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270b70(table: u32, index: u32) -> u32 {
    table
        .wrapping_add(index.wrapping_mul(SLOT_STRIDE as u32))
        .wrapping_add(SLOT_FIRST as u32)
}

/// Starts the periodic timer that drives the player table.
///
/// One-time setup: if the byte at `+0x00` is clear, create the `OSAlarm` at
/// `+0x08`, read the time base, compute the period and install
/// `0x80270C20` as a periodic handler. The period is derived from the bus clock:
///
/// ```text
/// ticks  = OS_BUS_CLOCK / 500000            // 486 on a 243 MHz bus
/// period = (ticks * 0x65B9AB) / 8000        // 404813 ticks
/// ```
///
/// `// observed:` the instruction stream implements both divisions with
/// reciprocal multiplies: `0x431BDE83` followed by a right shift for
/// `bus / 500000`, and `0x10624DD3` followed by `>> 9` for `/ 8000`. The C
/// above is the same arithmetic.
///
/// The periodic setter takes (alarm, ticks, start-high, start-low, 0, period,
/// handler); the `0` is the high half of a 64-bit period whose low half is the
/// computed `period`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80270b80` at `0x80270B80` (156 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80270b80_FUN_80270b80.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `timer`: address of a small state object: byte `+0x00` = "already
///   started", `OSAlarm` at `+0x08`.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// - `OSCreateAlarm(timer + 8)`,
/// - `OSSetPeriodicAlarm(...)` with handler `0x80270C20`,
/// - writes `timer + 0x00 = 1`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `timer` must be writable for `0x38` bytes (alarm at `+8`).
///
/// # Confidence
/// medium - the arithmetic is cross-checked against the machine code, but the
/// semantic meaning of the two scaling constants (`500000`, `0x65B9AB`,
/// `8000`) is unknown.
pub unsafe fn fn_80270b80(timer: usize) {
    if platform::read_u8(timer) != 0 {
        return;
    }

    platform::call1(ALARM_INIT, (timer + 0x08) as u32);

    let now = call_u64(OS_GET_TIME);
    let bus = platform::read_u32(OS_BUS_CLOCK);
    // observed: `_DAT_800000f8 / 500000` in the C. The machine reaches it with
    // a reciprocal multiply (`0x431BDE83`) and shifts, giving 486 at 243 MHz.
    let ticks = bus / 500_000;
    // observed: `((bus / 500000) * 0x65b9ab) / 8000`.
    let period = ticks.wrapping_mul(0x0065_B9AB) / 8_000;

    call7(
        ALARM_SET_PERIODIC,
        (timer + 0x08) as u32,
        ticks,
        (now >> 32) as u32,
        now as u32,
        0,
        period,
        0x8027_0C20,
    );

    platform::write_u8(timer, 1);
}

// ===========================================================================
// Local call helper
//
// `crate::platform` provides every arity used here; only the result-preserving
// form of `call0` is missing, so it is defined once at the bottom.
// ===========================================================================

/// Calls a routine by absolute address with no arguments and keeps its result.
///
/// [`crate::platform::call0`] discards the return value; the player table, the
/// manager pointer and the interrupt lock all return a `u32` that matters.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr` must be the entry point of a function taking no arguments.
unsafe fn call0_u32(addr: usize) -> u32 {
    let f: extern "C" fn() -> u32 = core::mem::transmute(addr);
    f()
}

// ===========================================================================
// Gameplay player actor: pose, animation predicates and effects,
// 0x80048C40..0x8004CD20.
//
// This is a second, unrelated use of the word "player". The cluster above is
// the sound library's sequence player; the functions below read the gameplay
// actor (world position at `+0xAC`, animation controller at `+0x1EC`, flags at
// `+0x10D4`/`+0x10D8`). They were promoted from
// `src/subsystems/r80040_80080.rs` with their docs and `# Original` evidence
// unchanged; only the shared field constants were renamed to `ACTOR_*` so they
// cannot be confused with the sequence-player `SLOT_*` offsets above.
// ===========================================================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Gameplay actor field offsets used by more than one function.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// `float[3]` world position, handed to the effect spawner.
const ACTOR_POS: usize = 0x0AC;

/// Animation controller object passed to `0x80070720`/`0x80070820`/`0x80070760`.
const ACTOR_ANIM: usize = 0x1EC;

/// `u8` owning-player index (0..3); first argument of the effect spawner.
const ACTOR_OWNER: usize = 0x38D;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Local helpers for the gameplay-actor cluster.
//
// `platform` exposes unsigned byte/word accessors and `call0..call7`; these
// three helpers add signed-byte and IEEE-754 single access. The target is
// big-endian, and `platform::read_u32` already reassembles the word, so the
// `f32` conversions are exact.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Read a signed 8-bit field. The original uses `lbz` + `extsb` where the
/// value is then sign-extended (for example the player index).
#[inline]
unsafe fn read_i8(addr: usize) -> i8 {
    platform::read_u8(addr) as i8
}

/// Read an IEEE-754 single. The target is big-endian; `platform::read_u32`
/// already reassembles the word, so `from_bits` is exact.
#[inline]
unsafe fn read_f32(addr: usize) -> f32 {
    f32::from_bits(platform::read_u32(addr))
}

/// Write an IEEE-754 single as its raw big-endian bits.
#[inline]
unsafe fn write_f32(addr: usize, value: f32) {
    platform::write_u32(addr, value.to_bits());
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 0x80048C40 - spawn the "miss" hit effect
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Spawns the player's `Wm_mr_misshit` effect at the actor position.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80048C40` at `0x80048C40` (40 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80048c40_FUN_80048c40.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `actor`: player object. `+0x38D` is the owning-player index; `+0xAC` is
///   the position passed to the effect system.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing (the original tail-calls the effect spawner, so its result is the
/// caller's result but is not used).
///
/// # Side effects
/// `0x8008FB60(player, "Wm_mr_misshit", 0, position, 0, 0)`. The callee stores
/// the player index in an SDA global, calls the effect system `0x8016C890`,
/// and restores the global to `-1`.
///
